WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.200
I'm at the summit of Mount Evans
in Colorado, USA.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.940
It is one of the Colorado Fourteeners,

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.550
meaning I'm more than
14,000 feet above sea level.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:10.620
As Americans measure it.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.740
For the rest of the world,

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:14.900
that is 4.3 kilometres above sea level,

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.960
or about half way to an
airplane's cruising altitude.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.560
And the air up here is thin:

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.140
I'm only getting about 60% of
the oxygen that I normally do

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.140
each time I breathe in.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.730
And let me tell you, this
was a difficult climb.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.880
At least, the last bit of it was.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.589
Because this is also the most
American mountain,

00:00:29.589 --> 00:00:31.929
becacuse there is a road to the top of it.

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:34.280
The highest paved road in North America.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:39.140
And this summit here counts as the
41st highest mountain in North America.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.300
But how do we count that?

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.390
Just over there, the wonderfully named
Meyer-Womble Observatory

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.300
is on another, slightly lower summit.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:47.830
But it's surrounded by lower ground:

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:50.210
why doesn't that count as the 42nd highest?

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:51.780
And you might say, well,

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.280
the answer's obvious,
it's part of the same mountain.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.070
But where does that end?

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:59.460
This summit, Mount Evans, is just
a smaller part of a big mountain range,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.290
so you could try and make the case
that this is just a bump

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.479
on the way to the tallest peak in the Rockies,
Mount Elbert.

00:01:04.479 --> 00:01:06.740
Or you could take it to the extreme and say

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.810
that there's only one mountain
on each land mass,

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:11.969
that every other peak in North America is
just a small bump

00:01:11.969 --> 00:01:15.200
on the way to the enormous continent-sized
mountain called Denali.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.060
Now that's ridiculous, most people would say.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.060
It's obvious.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.060
That's a mountain.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:21.060
And that isn't.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.119
But in formal terms, we solve it
by measuring prominence.

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:25.179
Prominence works like this:

00:01:25.179 --> 00:01:28.650
imagine if you could raise sea level up
to where I'm sitting now.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.249
How many meters would it have to drop
before you could connect

00:01:31.249 --> 00:01:33.780
this mountain to another, taller one

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.219
and walk there without getting your feet wet?

00:01:36.219 --> 00:01:39.579
For Mount Evans, that is 839m down,

00:01:39.579 --> 00:01:42.210
or about one-fifth of its height
above sea level.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.109
That observatory summit?

00:01:44.109 --> 00:01:45.109
It's not prominent.

00:01:45.109 --> 00:01:47.549
Sure, you have to walk downhill from it,
but after a few meters

00:01:47.549 --> 00:01:49.279
you're going uphill again.

00:01:49.279 --> 00:01:50.939
So where do you draw the line?

00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:54.430
Well, there are several different lines,
because of course there are.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:58.099
The International Climbing and
Mountaineering Federation defines a mountain

00:01:58.099 --> 00:02:00.630
as needing 300m of prominence,

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:02.979
which is actually only the height
of the Eiffel Tower,

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:05.240
which... doesn't seem like much to me?

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.399
Other folks disagree,
and say it should be 500m,

00:02:07.399 --> 00:02:10.680
or that prominence should
change with altitude...

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:11.680
it's a mess.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.430
I'll be honest, even after researching this,
I'm not sure about it.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.630
Even the folks I asked
to check my script went,

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:21.670
"eh, well, I guess, basically, but technically..."

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:26.210
This is one of those times that the human need
to categorise and catalogue everything

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.200
runs up against messy reality.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:34.450
There are always edge cases, and pretty much
every mountain is an edge case.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.080
I'm going to go back down now before I get
altitude sickness(!)

